{
  "id": "urn:visionflow:page:cross-chain-asset-transfer",
  "title": "Cross Chain Asset Transfer",
  "slug": "cross-chain-asset-transfer",
  "public": true,
  "classIri": "urn:ngm:class:cross-chain-asset-transfer",
  "domain": "blockchain",
  "definition": "Cross-chain asset transfer is the cryptographic and protocol-level process of moving digital assets \u2014 cryptocurrencies, fungible tokens, or non-fungible tokens \u2014 from one blockchain network to a distinct, independent blockchain network while preserving the asset's economic properties and enforcing integrity guarantees across the transfer. Because independent blockchains maintain no shared global state, protocols must ensure that an asset locked or burned on the source chain is atomically minted or released on the destination chain, preventing double-spend and maintaining supply conservation. Implementations range from hash time-locked contracts (HTLCs) and lock-and-mint bridge contracts overseen by validator committees to native inter-blockchain communication protocols (IBC) and zero-knowledge proof-based verification bridges that eliminate trusted attestors entirely.",
